{"id": "section-body-14110", "title": "Acute Retinal Vascular Occlusions", "chapterId": "chapter-fourteen", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "EYE DISEASES AND REFRACTIVE ERRORS", "Major Blinding Eye Diseases", "Acute Retinal Vascular Occlusions"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-14110", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Acute Retinal Vascular Occlusions", "runs": [{"text": "Acute"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Retinal"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Vascular"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Occlusions"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1312"}}, {"id": "body-14111", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Acute retinal vascular occlusions are common causes of visual impairment. Two forms exist retinal artery occlusions (RAOs) and retinal vein occlusions (RVOs). They are both associated with increased age and cardiovascular risk factors. These are synonyms to CVA of the eye. Acute management of RAOs involves a multidisciplinary approach including neurologists with stroke expertise, while RVO treatment is provided by ophthalmologists; optimization of systemic risk factors by patients’ primary care providers is an important component of RAO and RVO management.", "runs": [{"text": "Acute retinal vascular occlusions are common causes of visual impairment. Two forms exist retinal artery occlusions (RAOs) and retinal vein occlusions (RVOs). They are both associated with increased age and cardiovascular risk factors. These are synonyms to CVA of the eye. Acute management of RAOs involves a multidisciplinary approach including neurologists with stroke"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "expertise,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "while"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "RVO"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "treatment"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "provided"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "by"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ophthalmologists;"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "optimization"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {